      <section class="section prose" aria-label="About GridPaw">
        <h2>What is <span class="accent">GridPaw</span>? — Free Akari Puzzle Game</h2>
        <p>GridPaw is a <strong>free Light Up puzzle game</strong> with charming cats. Your goal is to place glowing cats on the grid so that every empty cell is illuminated, while respecting the numbered clues. It's the same satisfying logic as <strong>Akari puzzles</strong> — a classic Japanese brain teaser — but wrapped in an adorable feline theme.</p>
        <p>If you're looking for a place to <strong>play akari online</strong> free, you're already there: every level below runs right in your browser, no download and no sign up.</p>
        <p>Whether you're a puzzle veteran or completely new to <strong>Akari</strong>, GridPaw offers a gentle learning curve: start with the Tutorial, work through Easy boards, and graduate to Expert and Master sizes up to 12×12. Every level is hand-crafted and solvable with pure logic — no guessing required.</p>

        <h3>How Akari Puzzles Work</h3>
        <p>The rules of <strong>Akari</strong> (Light Up) are simple to learn but hard to master:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Place lights (glowing cats) on empty cells.</li>
          <li>Lights illuminate their entire row and column until blocked by a wall.</li>
          <li>No two lights may shine on each other — if two cats can see each other in the same row or column, that's illegal.</li>
          <li>Numbered cells indicate exactly how many lights must be placed directly adjacent (up, down, left, right).</li>
          <li>The puzzle is solved when every empty cell is lit and every numbered clue is satisfied.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>How to <span class="accent">Play</span> Akari Puzzle — GridPaw Guide</h2>
        <p>Tap any empty cell to place a glowing cat. Tap again to remove it. The top bar shows your remaining lives — make an invalid move and you'll lose one. Use the Hint button when you're stuck: it reveals the next logical cell without solving the entire board.</p>
        <p><strong>Double-click</strong> (or right-click / long-press on mobile) to mark a cell with a paw print — useful for noting cells you know can't have cats. This note-taking mechanic is essential for harder puzzles where you need to track multiple possibilities.</p>
        <p>Each difficulty introduces gradually bigger boards: 5×5 for Tutorial, 6×6 for Easy, up to 12×12 for Master. Larger boards require more advanced <strong>Akari</strong> techniques, including edge reasoning and exclusion along walls, but the core mechanic never changes.</p>

        <h2>Akari Puzzle <span class="accent">Difficulty Levels</span> Explained</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Tutorial (5×5)</strong> — Perfect for first-timers. Small grid, simple clues, quick wins. Teaches every rule step by step.</li>
          <li><strong>Easy (6×6)</strong> — Great for building confidence. More numbered clues, straightforward deduction.</li>
          <li><strong>Medium (7×7)</strong> — The standard challenge. Requires intermediate techniques like line-of-sight elimination.</li>
          <li><strong>Medium-Hard (8×8)</strong> — Mixed layouts requiring careful scanning and cross-hatching.</li>
          <li><strong>Hard (9×9)</strong> — For experienced puzzlers. Fewer clues, deeper reasoning needed.</li>
          <li><strong>Expert (10×10)</strong> — Misplacement causes cascading errors. Plan before you place.</li>
          <li><strong>Master (12×12)</strong> — The ultimate challenge for the most demanding solvers.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Tips &amp; <span class="accent">Strategy</span> for Akari Puzzles</h2>
        <p>Rushing to fill cells is the fastest way to lose lives. Instead, work systematically:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Start with the 4s.</strong> A wall cell numbered 4 forces a light in every adjacent cell — no other option. Place them immediately.</li>
          <li><strong>Use 0s next.</strong> A 0 means none of its neighbors can have cats. Mark them all with paw prints right away.</li>
          <li><strong>Watch for "dead cells."</strong> If a cell can't be lit from any direction after a placement, that placement is wrong.</li>
          <li><strong>Mark uncertain candidates.</strong> Use the paw print mark to track possibilities before committing.</li>
          <li><strong>Validate lines, not cells.</strong> Check entire rows and columns for conflicts before finalizing a section.</li>
          <li><strong>Look for forced placements.</strong> If a cell can only be lit from one direction, that's a forced cat position.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Once you internalize these patterns, you'll notice that advanced <strong>Akari</strong> boards are rarely about searching — they're about eliminating impossible positions until only one solution remains. Visit our <a href="/akari/tips/">Tips &amp; Strategy</a> page for detailed guides on each technique.</p>

        <h2><span class="accent">Why</span> Play Akari Puzzle Online at GridPaw?</h2>
        <p>Logic puzzles like GridPaw are a relaxing way to train pattern recognition and deductive reasoning. Because the rules are compact and visual, each session stays lightweight — perfect for a coffee break — while the larger boards reward deliberate, layered thinking.</p>
        <p>Unlike many puzzle games, GridPaw has <strong>no timers, no ads interrupting gameplay, and no paywalls</strong>. Every puzzle is free, every solution is unique, and every successfully lit room ends with a delightful feline flourish. Plus, the <a href="/akari/daily/">Daily Challenge</a> gives you a reason to come back every day with a fresh 10×10 puzzle — and a 14×14 Weekend Super Challenge on Saturdays and Sundays.</p>
        <p>If you enjoy Sudoku, KenKen, or other number logic puzzles, <strong>Akari</strong> offers a refreshing change of pace with spatial reasoning instead of arithmetic. And if you're already an Akari fan, GridPaw's cat theme adds a layer of charm that makes every solve feel like a small celebration.</p>

        <h3>Common Akari Puzzle Mistakes to Avoid</h3>
        <p>New <strong>Akari puzzle</strong> players often make the same few mistakes. <strong>Forgetting line-of-sight:</strong> two cats in the same row or column without a wall between them is always illegal — the game will warn you with a shake animation. <strong>Ignoring number constraints:</strong> a numbered cell's count is exact, not "at most." A "2" needs exactly two cats adjacent, no more and no fewer. <strong>Guessing instead of deducing:</strong> every Akari puzzle in GridPaw has a unique solution that can be reached by pure logic. If you're guessing, you're missing a clue — try the Hint button to find the next logical step.</p>

        <h3>Akari Puzzle Tips for Advanced Players</h3>
        <p>Once you've mastered the basics, try these advanced <strong>Akari puzzle</strong> strategies. <strong>Corner tactics:</strong> a "2" in a corner means both adjacent cells must have cats — no guesswork needed. <strong>Forced lighting:</strong> if a white cell can only be reached by one possible cat position, that position is forced. <strong>Number propagation:</strong> when you know a number already has enough adjacent cats, mark its remaining neighbors as empty. <strong>Wall scanning:</strong> check each wall segment — if a row has only one unlit cell between two walls, that cell must hold a cat. These techniques are essential for Hard and Expert Akari puzzle grids.</p>

        <h3>Why Akari Puzzles Are Good for Your Brain</h3>
        <p>Playing <strong>Akari puzzles</strong> regularly exercises your logical reasoning and spatial awareness. Unlike passive entertainment, each Akari puzzle requires active deduction — you must analyze constraints, eliminate possibilities, and commit to decisions. Studies suggest that logic puzzles like Akari help maintain cognitive sharpness, similar to how crosswords exercise vocabulary. The best part? GridPaw's cat theme makes the mental workout feel like play, not work. Whether you have five minutes or an hour, there's always an Akari puzzle that fits your schedule and skill level.</p>
      </section>

